#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097612} :activity/id #uuid "71e9c379-9817-5da3-85f9-50847626fa5d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050787 :topic/id #uuid "67094ea1-3fa3-574a-a9fb-47317689906e" :topic/math-academy-id 1043} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097798 :tutorial/content "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad\ny=2t-t^3, \\qquad\nt \\in  (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1,$ as shown. \n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y(t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{3}-3{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-3(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d0c890f-fbe0-5ded-948b-b94d13caac3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1083 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "786032d8-d66a-579e-8336-348ca609cfb8" :step/math-academy-id 630055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096538 :question/id #uuid "85879186-0866-5159-905b-65d043681231" :question/math-academy-id "e-604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534062}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "296a64b2-a271-5186-bf37-e47d7b56624c" :step/math-academy-id 630060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096561 :question/id #uuid "95cdc31d-c3f2-50b6-9447-b7317af775f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7412"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534099}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "3a032caf-6330-5e24-bafb-d45c348ad375" :step/math-academy-id 630057 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097915 :tutorial/content "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! \n\nTo see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0.$ However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\ny\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0\n$$\n\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "89e96b74-e406-52f5-b64e-46267df927e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7915 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "97757635-74ae-5269-ac75-2ea85021adb9" :step/math-academy-id 630056 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097653}}] :activity/title "Calculating Areas Bounded by Parametric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096851}]}
